# Documentation for the three shipped example datasets.
#
# WHAT THEY ARE FOR. Every example and vignette in the package needs data, and
# constructing a network inline at the top of each one buries the point of the
# example under twenty lines of `expand.grid()`. These exist so that an example
# can be about the function it documents.
#
# ALL THREE ARE SYNTHETIC AND rnetis'S OWN, generated by
# `data-raw/demo-datasets.R`, which reproduces them exactly. Nothing here is
# anyone else's data, so no redistribution question attaches to any of it.
#
# EACH CARRIES ITS `truth`, where a generating process defined one. That is the
# reason to ship generated data rather than a hand-typed table: an example can
# fit a model and compare the estimate against the value that produced it,
# which is a demonstration that the function works rather than only that it
# runs.

#' Example Influence data: one network, two waves
#'
#' A single group of 30 actors observed at two waves, generated by
#' [simulate_influence()] with a known autoregression and a known exposure
#' effect. The commonest shape in the package: two waves, so the one transition a
#' fit analyses gives one outcome row per actor.
#'
#' @format A [netis_data()] object with 30 actors, 2 waves, one group.
#'   Node variables: `y` (the outcome) and `x` (a covariate). One tie variable,
#'   `tie`.
#'
#'   The `truth` attribute records the parameters that generated it --
#'   `attr(demo_influence, "truth")` -- so an example can check an estimate
#'   against them.
#' @source Simulated by `data-raw/demo-datasets.R` (seed 20260101). Synthetic;
#'   not derived from any real dataset.
#' @seealso [demo_influence_groups] for the multi-group case,
#'   [demo_selection] for a tie outcome, [fit_influence()]
#' @examples
#' demo_influence
#' attr(demo_influence, "truth")
"demo_influence"

#' Example Influence data: four networks, two waves
#'
#' Four independent groups of unequal size (12, 9, 14 and 7 actors) observed at
#' two waves, with a group-level offset so that between-group variance is
#' genuinely present.
#'
#' @section Why the groups are unequal:
#' Deliberately, and it matters for what this dataset can show. With equal
#' group sizes the three `n_j` conventions [icc2()] offers -- `n0`, harmonic
#' and arithmetic -- give the same answer, so a balanced dataset cannot
#' demonstrate the difference the choice makes. It also means a reader cannot
#' see that the choice exists.
#'
#' @format A [netis_data()] object with 42 actors across 4 groups, 2 waves.
#'   Node variables: `y` and `x`. One tie variable, `tie`, observed at wave 1
#'   only: the network enters a fit as the lagged conduit of the 1 -> 2
#'   transition, and the network at wave 2 is unobserved rather than a copy of
#'   wave 1's.
#' @source Simulated by `data-raw/demo-datasets.R` (seed 20260102). Synthetic;
#'   not derived from any real dataset.
#' @seealso [demo_influence], [icc2()], [influence_se_comparison()]
#' @examples
#' demo_influence_groups
#' # Node rows per group: actors times two waves.
#' table(netis_nodes(demo_influence_groups)$group)
"demo_influence_groups"

#' Example Selection data: one network, two waves, a tie outcome
#'
#' Twenty actors and every ordered pair between them, observed at two waves. A
#' tie's probability rises with the sender's and the receiver's `tenure`, and
#' strongly with whether the tie existed at the previous wave -- so
#' [sender()], [receiver()] and [prior_tie()] all have something to estimate.
#'
#' @section Zeros are measured, and the data says so:
#' Every ordered pair has a row at every wave, so a `0` is an observed
#' non-tie rather than a missing observation. That is declared in the object
#' (`zero_meaning = "measured_zero"`), because rnetis does not infer what a zero
#' means: a sparse edge list and a complete dyadic table look alike and mean
#' different things.
#'
#' @format A [netis_data()] object with 20 actors, 380 ordered pairs per wave,
#'   2 waves, one group. Node variable: `tenure`. Tie variable: `tie`.
#'
#'   The `truth` attribute records the latent-scale coefficients that generated
#'   it, for comparison against a fit.
#' @source Simulated by `data-raw/demo-datasets.R` (seed 20260103). Synthetic;
#'   not derived from any real dataset.
#' @seealso [fit_selection()], [demo_influence]
#' @examples
#' demo_selection
#' attr(demo_selection, "truth")
"demo_selection"
